/*
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Node disk probe — diagnostic only, OPT-IN
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Logs each Batch node's disk usage at the start of EVERY task, so "256 GB is enough"
    becomes a measured margin instead of an inference. See
    docs/dev-practices/azure_batch_execution.md §9.

        nextflow run ... -c conf/disk_probe.config
        tw launch ... --config conf/disk_probe.config

    Deliberately NOT a profile — pass it with -c/--config so the blast radius is exactly
    the runs that opt in, matching conf/azure_batch.config.

    WHY TWO df TARGETS. A Batch node has two disks doing different jobs, and only one of
    them ever filled:

        /       OS / boot disk  — holds /var/lib/docker (image layers, overlay2).
                                  This is the one that hit DiskFull; enlarged to 256 GB.
        .       the task work dir — normally /mnt/batch/tasks on the 150 GB ephemeral
                                  NVMe. Under Fusion this is a FUSE mount instead, which
                                  is itself worth seeing.

    Reporting both is what makes the output interpretable: it says WHICH disk is under
    pressure, not merely that something is full.

    ⚠️ `beforeScript` runs INSIDE the task container, not on the node. `.command.run`
    re-invokes itself within `docker run`, and beforeScript lives in `.command.run` — so
    `hostname` returns a container id and `/` shows as `overlay`. Measured 2026-08-07.
    **The reading is still valid**: overlay2's `df` reports the BACKING filesystem, so
    `/` gives the node's OS disk (246.9 G observed on a 256 GB disk), which is the number
    we are after.

    ⚠️ **Under Fusion the work-dir `df` is meaningless** — Fusion presents a synthetic
    filesystem (`fusion 8.0P 4.0P 4.0P 50% /fusion`). Only the `root:` line is usable on a
    Fusion CE.

    ⚠️ **The `mnt :` line duplicates `root:`** — a Docker task container has no `/mnt` bind,
    so in-container `df /mnt` falls back to `/` (measured 2026-08-13). The host's ephemeral
    disk shows up only in the `work:` line, because the task sandbox (/mnt/batch/tasks)
    lives on it. The line is kept in case a future executor DOES bind /mnt.

    WHEN TO ATTACH. Cheap standing monitoring: add it to FUSION DEV RUNS (does Fusion's
    cache pressure the OS disk? the root: line answers that — see
    docs/dev-practices/azure_batch_execution.md §9) and to any run after ADDING A TOOL
    (does the image set creep past the measured ~15 G?). Skip it on runs you intend to
    resume, or whose task cache must match a no-probe run — the hash perturbation above.

    ⚠️ This may perturb the task hash (beforeScript lives in .command.run, not
    .command.sh). Assume a run with this config cannot reuse — or be reused by — a cached
    run without it. Fine for a diagnostic run; do not leave it on for one you intend to
    resume.

    HARVESTING. Output lands in each task's .command.log in the work dir on blob:

        az storage blob download -c <workdir-container> \
            --name "<workdir-path>/<xx>/<hash>/.command.log" \
            --file /tmp/x.log --account-name <acct> --auth-mode login
        grep disk-probe /tmp/x.log

    Sample ~20 tasks spread across the run rather than all ~170.
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
*/

process.beforeScript = '''
echo "[disk-probe] host=$(hostname) root: $(df -h /    | tail -1)"
echo "[disk-probe] host=$(hostname) work: $(df -h .    | tail -1)"
echo "[disk-probe] host=$(hostname) mnt : $(df -h /mnt 2>/dev/null | tail -1)"
'''
